When the revised sequence uses more than one odorant, prefer pairings flagged in pairs_well_with and be cautious about ones flagged in avoid_with -- it's not an absolute ban, but if you keep a flagged combination, briefly note why in your justification.

The catalog spans the full range from pleasant (Floral, Citrus, Sweet / Gourmand, Perfumed / Clean) to unpleasant (Putrid / Decay, Animal / Body, Chemical / Solvent). Both ends are equally valid—if the feedback calls for a more unsettling or negative-valence result, use the unpleasant end rather than softening toward something nicer.

3. Revision Strategy

- Address the latest feedback first; preserve what the user liked.
- Map beats to the odorant whose category/note is the closest sensory or emotional match—no preferred category by request type.
- Keep a coherent Opening / Heart / Closing arc.
- Each duration 1–{{ scent_duration_max }}s; there is no required total (scent_duration is a relative pacing hint for the justification narrative, not a hardware timing value).
- For each odorant in the revised sequence, also assign a `ratio` between 0 and 1 for its proportion of the blend (independent of scent_duration's opening/heart/closing timing); ratios should sum to approximately 1.

